1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,937
Chapter 20: Antepartum Fetal Assessment.

3
00:00:05,937 --> 00:00:26,400
Fetal Movements Fetal Breathing Contraction Stress Testing Nonstress Tests Biophysical Profile Amnionic Fluid Volume Doppler Velocimetry Antenatal Testing Summary References Techniques employed to evaluate fetal health focus on fetal biophysical findings that include heart rate, movement, breathing, and amnionic fluid production.

4
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:36,025
Antepartum fetal surveillance aims to prevent fetal death in pregnancies with complex maternal and fetal conditions yet avoid unnecessary interventions .

5
00:00:36,025 --> 00:00:42,112
Most fetuses will be healthy, and a normal antepartum test result is highly reassuring.

6
00:00:42,112 --> 00:00:46,362
Fetal death within 1 week of a normal test result is rare.

7
00:00:46,362 --> 00:00:56,562
Indeed, negative predictive values—true negative test results for fetal jeopardy—for most of the tests described are 99.8 percent or higher.

8
00:00:56,562 --> 00:01:06,300
In contrast, estimates of positive predictive values—true positive test results for fetal jeopardy— are low and range between 10 and 40 percent.

9
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:11,625
The benefit of fetal surveillance is primarily based on circumstantial evidence.

10
00:01:11,625 --> 00:01:17,537
No definitive randomized clinical trials have been conducted for obvious ethical reasons .

11
00:01:17,537 --> 00:01:19,462
Fetal Movements.

12
00:01:19,462 --> 00:01:22,437
# Fetal Behavioral States.

13
00:01:22,437 --> 00:01:31,125
Fetal activity commences as early as 7 weeks’ gestation (Sajapala, 2017; Vindla, 1995).

14
00:01:31,125 --> 00:01:39,612
Between 20 and 30 weeks’ gestation, general body movements become organized, and the fetus starts to show rest-activity cycles .

15
00:01:39,612 --> 00:01:44,537
These cycles reflect central nervous system development and maturation.

16
00:01:44,537 --> 00:01:52,637
By approximately 36 weeks’ gestation, restactivity cycles give way to behavioral states in most normal fetuses .

17
00:01:52,637 --> 00:02:04,975
Four fetal behavioral states are described by Nijhuis and coworkers (1982): • State 1F is a quiescent state—quiet sleep—with a narrow oscillatory bandwidth of the fetal heart rate.

18
00:02:04,975 --> 00:02:12,962
State 2F includes frequent gross body movements, continuous eye movements, and wider oscillation of the fetal heart rate.

19
00:02:12,962 --> 00:02:18,587
This state is analogous to rapid eye movement (REM) or active sleep in the neonate.

20
00:02:18,587 --> 00:02:25,787
State 3F includes continuous eye movements in the absence of body movements and no heart rate accelerations.

21
00:02:25,787 --> 00:02:29,550
This state is rare, and its existence is disputed .

22
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:36,225
State 4F is one of vigorous body movement with continuous eye movements and heart rate accelerations.

23
00:02:36,225 --> 00:02:40,087
This state corresponds to the awake state in newborns.

24
00:02:40,087 --> 00:02:54,100
At 28 to 30 weeks’ gestation, fetuses transition to spend most of their time in states 1F and 2F, namely, in quiet or active sleep (Peirano 2003; Suwanrath, 2010).

25
00:02:54,100 --> 00:03:00,212
For example, at 38 weeks, 75 percent of time is spent in these two states.

26
00:03:00,212 --> 00:03:07,062
These behavioral states have been used to develop an increasingly sophisticated understanding of fetal behavior.

27
00:03:07,062 --> 00:03:20,075
In a study of fetal urine production, bladder volumes rose during state 1F quiet sleep and significantly declined during state 2F active sleep due to diminished urine production and infrequent fetal voiding .

28
00:03:20,075 --> 00:03:25,475
These phenomena were thought to represent reduced renal blood flow during active sleep.

29
00:03:25,475 --> 00:03:27,775
Figure 20-1.

30
00:03:27,775 --> 00:03:34,975
The change in fetal sleep patterns (blue columns) and corresponding number of accelerations (graph lines).

31
00:03:34,975 --> 00:03:43,150
As the third trimester advances, fetuses spend more time in active sleep and the number of accelerations per hour increases.

32
00:03:43,150 --> 00:03:46,412
# Determinants of Fetal Activity.

33
00:03:46,412 --> 00:03:53,875
One determinant of fetal activity is the just-described sleep-awake cycles, which are independent of maternal ones.

34
00:03:53,875 --> 00:04:02,987
In one study of 16 near-term fetuses, the mean duration of a complete cycle, which included quiet and active states, was 60 minutes.

35
00:04:02,987 --> 00:04:09,137
The mean duration was 23 minutes for the quiet states and 40 minutes for the active states .

36
00:04:09,137 --> 00:04:23,862
Patrick and associates (1982) measured gross fetal body movements with real-time sonography for 24-hour periods in 31 normal near-term pregnancies and found the longest period of inactivity to be 75 minutes.

37
00:04:23,862 --> 00:04:28,662
Amnionic fluid volume is another factor affecting fetal activity.

38
00:04:28,662 --> 00:04:41,225
Sherer and colleagues (1996) assessed the number of fetal movements in relation to amnionic fluid volume in 465 preterm pregnancies during biophysical profile testing.

39
00:04:41,225 --> 00:04:44,225
Fetal activity declined in those with

40
00:04:44,380 --> 00:04:52,392
diminished amnionic volumes, and the authors suggested that a restricted uterine space might physically limit fetal movements.

41
00:04:52,342 --> 00:04:56,192
Patient habits and medications alter fetal movement.

42
00:04:56,192 --> 00:05:04,105
For example, maternal smoking decreases fetal activity (Coppens, 2001; Thaler, 1980).

43
00:05:04,105 --> 00:05:14,330
Treatment of substance abuse disorders with methadone and buprenorphine also reduces fetal movement (Jansson, 2017; Wouldes, 2004).

44
00:05:14,330 --> 00:05:27,092
Betamethasone administration is associated with decreased fetal movement for 24 to 72 hours, and the diurnal pattern is lost (Koenen, 2005; Mulder, 2009).

45
00:05:27,092 --> 00:05:30,442
The effect is less clear with dexamethasone.

46
00:05:30,442 --> 00:05:41,380
Maternal acetaminophen ingestion does not alter fetal movement, but a glucose load does promote activity (Aladjem, 1979; Nitsche, 2015).

47
00:05:41,380 --> 00:05:44,030
# Maternal Perception.

48
00:05:44,030 --> 00:05:56,855
Sadovsky and coworkers (1979b) classified fetal movements as weak, strong, and rolling according to maternal perceptions and independent recordings using piezoelectric sensors.

49
00:05:56,855 --> 00:06:03,692
As pregnancy advances, the rate of weak movements drops, and more vigorous ones rise in frequency.

50
00:06:03,692 --> 00:06:06,330
The latter then subside at term.

51
00:06:06,330 --> 00:06:12,780
Presumably, declining amnionic fluid volume and space account for diminished activity at term.

52
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:21,192
Figure 20-2 shows fetal movements during the last half of gestation in 127 pregnancies with normal outcomes.

53
00:06:21,192 --> 00:06:33,892
The mean number of weekly movements calculated from 12-hour daily recording periods rose from approximately 200 at 20 weeks’ gestation to a maximum of 575 movements at 32 weeks.

54
00:06:33,892 --> 00:06:39,855
Weekly fetal movement counts then declined to an average of 282 at 40 weeks.

55
00:06:39,855 --> 00:06:46,405
Another study of nearly 300 gravidas found a similar movement pattern across gestational ages .

56
00:06:46,405 --> 00:06:52,192
More than 90 percent of these women reported stronger movements during the evening and nighttime.

57
00:06:52,192 --> 00:06:54,367
Figure 20-2.

58
00:06:54,367 --> 00:07:01,255
Graph depicts averages of fetal movements counted during 12-hour periods (mean ± SEM).

59
00:07:01,255 --> 00:07:05,105
Data from Sadovsky, 1979a.

60
00:07:05,105 --> 00:07:10,880
Women perceive 16 to 45 percent of fetal movements detected by sonography .

61
00:07:10,880 --> 00:07:20,417
A higher body mass index does not decrease maternal perception of fetal movement (Bradford, 2019a; Sasson, 2016).

62
00:07:20,417 --> 00:07:32,030
It remains unclear if nulliparity or anterior placental location affect maternal impression of fetal activity (Brown, 2016; Sasson, 2016).

63
00:07:32,030 --> 00:07:34,830
# Clinical Application.

64
00:07:34,830 --> 00:07:48,842
Because women may perceive a decline in fetal movement in the days to weeks preceding stillbirth, maternal perception of fetal movement has been evaluated as a preventive aid (Heazell, 2008; Stacey, 2011).

65
00:07:48,842 --> 00:07:53,592
However, the optimal fetal-movement protocol remains undefined .

66
00:07:53,592 --> 00:08:02,230
Some methods rely on quantitative counts, such as 10 movements in 2 hours, whereas others rely on a mother’s subjective impression.

67
00:08:02,230 --> 00:08:12,430
In one study, more than 68,000 pregnancies were randomly assigned between 28 and 32 weeks’ gestation to an objective or subjective assessment group .

68
00:08:12,430 --> 00:08:18,380
Women in the objective arm were instructed to record the time needed to feel 10 movements each day.

69
00:08:18,380 --> 00:08:22,417
This required an average of 2.7 hours daily.

70
00:08:22,417 --> 00:08:27,967
Women in the subjective group were informally asked about movements during prenatal visits.

71
00:08:27,967 --> 00:08:33,480
Reports of decreased fetal motion were then evaluated with tests of fetal well-being.

72
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:44,242
Antepartum death rates for normal singleton fetuses were similar in the two study groups, and most stillborn fetuses were dead by the time the mothers reported for medical attention.

73
00:08:44,242 --> 00:08:54,567
Rather than concluding that maternal perceptions were meaningless, the authors concluded that informal maternal impressions were as valid as formally recorded fetal movement counts.

74
00:08:54,567 --> 00:08:58,092
Women’s knowledge of these protocols also vary.

75
00:08:58,092 --> 00:09:06,955
In one survey of more than 400 gravidas, 85 percent noted receiving instruction from their provider regarding normal fetal movement .

76
00:09:06,955 --> 00:09:14,342
However, 60 percent described it as a subjective assessment, and 40 percent reported a quantitative method.

77
00:09:14,342 --> 00:09:20,680
In another study, more than two thirds of women could not explain fetal movement monitoring techniques .

78
00:09:20,680 --> 00:09:27,580
Although a long-standing pillar of maternal care, fetal movement surveillance may not be as predictive as thought.

79
00:09:27,580 --> 00:09:35,917
Nearly 6 to 7 percent of pregnancies are complicated by decreased fetal movement, but stillbirth rates are not increased in these

80
00:09:36,076 --> 00:09:41,563
cases (Harrington, 1998; Scala, 2015).

81
00:09:41,513 --> 00:09:54,438
In one study of more than 400,000 pregnancies complicated by decreased fetal movement, directed patient and staff education coupled with a prescriptive management plan did not reduce the stillbirth incidence .

82
00:09:54,438 --> 00:10:06,463
Aside from stillbirth, some data show that decreased fetal movement may help identify growth-restricted fetuses before birth (Saastad, 2011; Scala, 2015).

83
00:10:06,463 --> 00:10:14,813
In one of these studies, nearly 1100 pregnancies were assigned to subjective or objective assessment of fetal activity.

84
00:10:14,813 --> 00:10:24,726
The rate of 1-minute Apgar scores ≤3 was significantly reduced (0.4 versus 2.3 percent) when counting was used .

85
00:10:24,726 --> 00:10:35,788
Thus, although most pregnancies complicated by decreased fetal movement will result in normal outcomes, maternal perception of reduced fetal activity warrants further evaluation.

86
00:10:35,788 --> 00:10:39,251
No consensus guides a provider’s response.

87
00:10:39,251 --> 00:10:45,276
Care is individualized and influenced by gestational age and pregnancy comorbidities.

88
00:10:45,276 --> 00:10:52,826
In low-risk pregnancies, documenting fetal heart tones and adequate amnionic fluid volume may be sufficient.

89
00:10:52,826 --> 00:10:58,513
For older fetuses, tests described in the upcoming sections are typically added.

90
00:10:58,513 --> 00:11:04,376
Our practice is a nonstress test and sonographic measurement of amnionic fluid volume.

91
00:11:04,376 --> 00:11:08,938
Many women report excessive fetal movement in the third trimester.

92
00:11:08,938 --> 00:11:12,463
Its risk, if any, is poorly understood.

93
00:11:12,463 --> 00:11:21,713
Stacey and colleagues (2011) reported that a single episode of vigorous fetal activity is associated with a greater risk for fetal death.

94
00:11:21,713 --> 00:11:34,663
Similarly, others have described maternal perception of a single episode of excessive of fetal movement in the weeks prior to a stillbirth diagnosis (Heazell, 2017; Whitehead, 2020).

95
00:11:34,663 --> 00:11:38,713
More research is needed prior to recommending intervention.

96
00:11:38,713 --> 00:11:40,538
Fetal Breathing.

97
00:11:40,538 --> 00:11:48,513
Small inward and outward flow of tracheal fluid, indicating thoracic movement, was first identified in fetal sheep .

98
00:11:48,513 --> 00:11:53,963
These chest wall movements differ from those following birth in that they are discontinuous.

99
00:11:53,963 --> 00:12:00,801
Another feature of fetal respiration is paradoxical chest wall movement ( Johnson, 1988).

100
00:12:00,801 --> 00:12:04,263
In the newborn or adult, the opposite occurs.

101
00:12:04,263 --> 00:12:10,976
One interpretation of paradoxical respiratory motion might be coughing to clear amnionic fluid debris.

102
00:12:10,976 --> 00:12:21,313
Although the physiological basis for the breathing reflex is not completely understood, such exchange of amnionic fluid appears to be essential for normal lung development .

103
00:12:21,313 --> 00:12:26,951
Dawes (1974) identified two types of respiratory movements.

104
00:12:26,951 --> 00:12:32,438
The first are gasps or sighs, which occurred at a frequency of 1 to 4 per minute.

105
00:12:32,438 --> 00:12:38,851
The second, irregular bursts of breathing, occurred at rates up to 240 cycles per minute.

106
00:12:38,851 --> 00:12:44,151
These latter rapid respiratory movements were associated with rapid eye movement.

107
00:12:44,151 --> 00:12:56,288
Badalian and associates (1993) studied the maturation of normal fetal breathing using color flow and spectral Doppler analysis of nasal fluid flow as an index of lung function.

108
00:12:56,288 --> 00:13:07,113
They suggested that fetal respiratory rate declined in conjunction with increasing respiratory volume at 33 to 36 weeks’ gestation and coincided with lung maturation.

109
00:13:07,113 --> 00:13:09,426
Figure 20-3.

110
00:13:09,426 --> 00:13:13,226
Paradoxical chest movement with fetal respiration.

111
00:13:13,226 --> 00:13:23,501
During inspiration (A), the chest wall paradoxically collapses and the abdomen protrudes, whereas during expiration (B), the chest wall expands.

112
00:13:23,501 --> 00:13:30,176
Many investigators have used sonography to determine whether chest wall movements might reflect fetal health.

113
00:13:30,176 --> 00:13:34,726
Several variables in addition to hypoxia affect these movements.

114
00:13:34,726 --> 00:13:49,476
These include maternal hypoglycemia, sound stimuli, cigarette smoking, amniocentesis, impending preterm labor, gestational age, fetal heart rate, and labor—during which it is normal for fetal respiration to cease.

115
00:13:49,476 --> 00:13:57,126
Because fetal breathing movements are episodic, interpretation of fetal health when respirations are absent may be difficult.

116
00:13:57,126 --> 00:14:08,601
Patrick and coworkers (1980) performed continuous 24-hour observation using sonography to characterize fetal breathing patterns during the last 10 weeks of pregnancy.

117
00:14:08,601 --> 00:14:15,738
A total of 1224 hours of fetal observation in 51 pregnancies were collected.

118
00:14:15,738 --> 00:14:17,963
Figure 20-4.

119
00:14:17,963 --> 00:14:22,051
displays the percentages of time spent breathing near term.

120
00:14:22,051 --> 00:14:24,888
Substantively diminished breathing

121
00:14:25,060 --> 00:14:28,360
during the night suggests a diurnal pattern.

122
00:14:28,310 --> 00:14:33,585
In addition, breathing activity is enhanced somewhat following maternal meals.

123
00:14:33,585 --> 00:14:47,085
Total absence of breathing was observed in some of these normal fetuses for up to 122 minutes, indicating that fetal evaluation to diagnose absent respiratory motion may require long periods of observation.

124
00:14:47,085 --> 00:14:49,310
Figure 20-4.

125
00:14:49,310 --> 00:14:55,785
The percentage of time spent breathing by 11 fetuses at 38 to 39 weeks’ gestation.

126
00:14:55,785 --> 00:15:00,360
Fetal breathing activity significantly increases after breakfast.

127
00:15:00,360 --> 00:15:08,260
Breathing activity diminished during the day and reached its minimum between 20:00 and 24:00 hours.

128
00:15:08,260 --> 00:15:16,047
The percentage of time spent breathing rose significantly between 04:00 and 07:00 hours, when mothers were asleep.

129
00:15:16,047 --> 00:15:31,072
Adapted with permission from Patrick J, Campbell K, Carmichael L, et al: Patterns of human fetal breathing during the last 10 weeks of pregnancy, Obstet Gynecol. 1980 Jul;56(1):24–30.

130
00:15:31,072 --> 00:15:39,535
The potential for breathing activity to be an important sole marker of fetal health is unfulfilled because of the multiple factors that affect breathing.

131
00:15:39,535 --> 00:15:45,147
Most clinical applications couple fetal breathing with other biophysical indices.

132
00:15:45,147 --> 00:15:52,060
For example, fetal breathing is one component of the biophysical profile (p. 389).

133
00:15:52,060 --> 00:15:54,472
Contraction Stress Test.

134
00:15:54,472 --> 00:16:02,535
During a uterine contraction, pressures generated by the myometrium exceed the collapsing pressure of the vessels coursing through it.

135
00:16:02,535 --> 00:16:07,297
This ultimately lowers blood flow to the placenta’s intervillous space.

136
00:16:07,297 --> 00:16:11,085
Brief periods of impaired oxygen exchange result.

137
00:16:11,085 --> 00:16:20,285
If uteroplacental pathology is present, oxygen exchange to the fetus is further diminished, and late fetal heart rate decelerations appear .

138
00:16:20,285 --> 00:16:27,872
These downward-sloping heartrate waveforms begin with the onset of the uterine contraction waveform or just beyond its acme.

139
00:16:27,872 --> 00:16:34,197
Instead, contractions may produce variable decelerations as a result of cord compression.

140
00:16:34,197 --> 00:16:40,547
This suggests oligohydramnios, which is often comorbid with placental insufficiency.

141
00:16:40,547 --> 00:16:53,272
Ray and associates (1972) used this concept in 66 complicated pregnancies and developed the oxytocin challenge test, which was later called the contraction stress test (CST).

142
00:16:53,272 --> 00:17:01,047
A positive test result, that is, an abnormal result, is uniform repetitive late fetal heart rate decelerations.

143
00:17:01,047 --> 00:17:10,985
In their study, the tests were repeated weekly, and the authors concluded that negative CST results, that is, normal results, forecasted fetal health.

144
00:17:10,985 --> 00:17:17,985
To perform the test, contractions are induced with either intravenous oxytocin or nipple stimulation.

145
00:17:17,985 --> 00:17:27,047
If at least three spontaneous contractions of 40 seconds or longer are present in a 10-minute span, no uterine stimulation is necessary .

146
00:17:27,047 --> 00:17:33,435
The fetal heart rate and uterine contractions are recorded simultaneously by external monitors.

147
00:17:33,435 --> 00:17:40,510
With oxytocin, a dilute intravenous infusion is used to establish a satisfactory contraction pattern .

148
00:17:40,510 --> 00:17:49,547
At Parkland Hospital, 20 units of oxytocin are mixed in 1 liter of Ringer solution and initiated at a rate of 6 mU/min.

149
00:17:49,547 --> 00:17:56,460
The rate is increased by 6 mU/min every 40 minutes to achieve the just-discussed contraction pattern.

150
00:17:56,460 --> 00:18:02,410
Nipple stimulation to induce uterine contractions for a CST is usually successful .

151
00:18:02,410 --> 00:18:09,122
One method involves a woman rubbing one nipple through her clothing for 2 minutes or until a contraction begins.

152
00:18:09,122 --> 00:18:15,447
This 2-minute nipple stimulation ideally will induce a pattern of three contractions per 10 minutes.

153
00:18:15,447 --> 00:18:23,335
If not, after a 5-minute rest interval, she is instructed to retry nipple stimulation to achieve the desired pattern.

154
00:18:23,335 --> 00:18:27,860
If this is unsuccessful, dilute oxytocin may be used.

155
00:18:27,860 --> 00:18:33,472
CST results are interpreted according to the criteria shown in Table 201.

156
00:18:33,472 --> 00:18:39,035
Disadvantageously, the average CST requires 90 minutes to complete.

157
00:18:39,035 --> 00:18:44,772
Compared with oxytocin, nipple stimulation shortens testing time and costs less.

158
00:18:44,772 --> 00:18:59,272
During a CST, some have reported unpredictable uterine hyperstimulation and fetal distress, whereas others did not find excessive activity to be harmful (Frager, 1987; Schellpfeffer, 1985).

159
00:18:59,272 --> 00:19:06,085
Relative contraindications to a CST include those conditions that contraindicate vaginal delivery.

160
00:19:06,085 --> 00:19:08,360
Table 20-1.

161
00:19:08,360 --> 00:19:12,622
Criteria for Interpretation of the Contraction Stress Test

162
00:19:12,772 --> 00:19:14,859
Nonstress Test.

163
00:19:14,809 --> 00:19:29,247
The nonstress test (NST) also is used to assess fetal well-being and employs the phenomenon of fetal heart rate acceleration in response to fetal movement (Freeman, 1975; Lee, 1975).

164
00:19:29,247 --> 00:19:35,722
During an NST, these accelerations are correlated with fetal movements perceived by the mother.

165
00:19:35,722 --> 00:19:44,772
Compared with a CST, an NST is easier to perform, and normal results can be used to further define false-positive CST results.

166
00:19:44,772 --> 00:19:54,559
Simplistically, the NST is primarily a test of fetal condition, and it differs from the CST, which is considered a test of uteroplacental function.

167
00:19:54,559 --> 00:20:01,259
Currently, NST is the most widely used primary testing method for assessment of fetal well-being.

168
00:20:01,259 --> 00:20:08,184
It has also been incorporated into the biophysical profile, discussed later (p. 389).

169
00:20:08,184 --> 00:20:11,334
# Fetal Heart Rate Acceleration.

170
00:20:11,334 --> 00:20:20,647
Autonomic influences are mediated by sympathetic or parasympathetic impulses from brainstem centers to normally raise or slow the fetal heart rate.

171
00:20:20,647 --> 00:20:39,159
The NST is based on the hypothesis that the heart rate of a fetus that is not acidemic as a result of hypoxia or neurological depression will temporarily accelerate in response to fetal movement (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019; Lee, 1975).

172
00:20:39,159 --> 00:20:44,822
Fetal movements during testing are identified by maternal perception and self-recorded.

173
00:20:44,822 --> 00:20:49,572
As hypoxia develops, fetal heart rate accelerations diminish .

174
00:20:49,572 --> 00:20:54,197
Gestational age influences acceleration of the fetal heart rate.

175
00:20:54,197 --> 00:21:01,797
Pillai and James (1990b) studied patterns of fetal heart rate acceleration during normal pregnancy.

176
00:21:01,797 --> 00:21:09,909
The percentage of body movements accompanied by accelerations and the amplitude of these waveforms both increase with gestational age .

177
00:21:09,909 --> 00:21:12,259
Figure 20-5.

178
00:21:12,259 --> 00:21:20,209
Percentage of fetuses with at least one acceleration of 15 bpm sustained for 15 seconds concurrent with fetal movement.

179
00:21:20,209 --> 00:21:33,784
Redrawn from Pillai M, James D: The development of fetal heart rate patterns during normal pregnancy, Obstet Gynecol. 1990 Nov;76(5 Pt 1):812–816.

180
00:21:33,784 --> 00:21:43,509
Accordingly, the National Institute of Child Health and Human Development Fetal Monitoring Workshop defined acceleration waveforms based on gestational age .

181
00:21:43,509 --> 00:21:59,122
In fetuses at or beyond 32 weeks’ gestation, the acceleration acme is ≥15 beats per minute (bpm) above the baseline rate, and the acceleration lasts ≥15 seconds but <2 minutes.

182
00:21:59,122 --> 00:22:09,459
Before 32 weeks, accelerations are defined as having a rise ≥10 bpm above baseline for ≥10 seconds.

183
00:22:09,459 --> 00:22:22,984
In one study of 188 normal fetuses at 25 and 28 weeks’ gestation, only 70 percent demonstrated the required ≥15 bpm during heart rate accelerations.

184
00:22:22,984 --> 00:22:27,109
Accelerations of 10 bpm occurred in 90 percent .

185
00:22:27,109 --> 00:22:41,209
Cousins and associates (2012) compared the Workshop criteria recommended before 32 weeks with the standard 15 bpm/15 second criteria in a randomized trial of 143 women.

186
00:22:41,209 --> 00:22:44,847
They found no differences in perinatal outcomes.

187
00:22:44,847 --> 00:22:48,747
# Reactive (Normal) Nonstress Tests.

188
00:22:48,747 --> 00:22:56,359
A normal NST is termed reactive and requires two or more accelerations within 20 minutes of beginning the test .

189
00:22:56,359 --> 00:23:00,597
Accelerations are accepted irrespective of fetal movement.

190
00:23:00,597 --> 00:23:07,197
Before concluding that a test is nonreactive, a 40-minute or longer tracing should be performed.

191
00:23:07,197 --> 00:23:10,722
This threshold accounts for fetal sleep cycles .

192
00:23:10,722 --> 00:23:21,684
Miller and coworkers (1996b) reviewed outcomes in fetuses with NST results that were considered nonreactive because only one acceleration was recorded.

193
00:23:21,684 --> 00:23:27,834
They concluded that one acceleration was as reliable as two in predicting healthy fetal status.

194
00:23:27,834 --> 00:23:38,872
NSTs are more likely to be reactive and have a shorter testing time in the evening compared with the morning (Babazadeh, 2005; Petrikovsky, 1996).

195
00:23:38,872 --> 00:23:41,234
Figure 20-6.

196
00:23:41,234 --> 00:23:43,859
Reactive nonstress test.

197
00:23:43,859 --> 00:23:52,597
Notice there are at least two fetal heart rate accelerations (arrows) of more than 15 beats/min for longer than 15 seconds.

198
00:23:52,597 --> 00:23:58,234
The black line reflects fetal heart rate, whereas the purple line reflects the mother’s.

199
00:23:58,234 --> 00:24:04,409
Loud external sounds have been used to startle the fetus and thereby provoke heart rate acceleration.

200
00:24:04,409 --> 00:24:12,622
A commercially available acoustic stimulator is positioned on the maternal abdomen, and a stimulus of 1 to 2 seconds is applied .

201
00:24:12,622 --> 00:24:16,484
This may be repeated up to three times for up to 3

202
00:24:16,636 --> 00:24:18,323
seconds .

203
00:24:18,273 --> 00:24:33,523
In a randomized trial of 113 women undergoing NST, vibroacoustic stimulation lowered the average testing time and incidence of nonreactive test results (Perez-Delboy, 2002; Turitz, 2012).

204
00:24:33,523 --> 00:24:43,111
One case report described a fetal tachyarrhythmia that was provoked with vibroacoustic stimulation in a fetus with known premature atrial contractions .

205
00:24:43,111 --> 00:24:52,323
Although a normal number and amplitude of accelerations seems to reflect fetal well-being, their absence does not invariably predict fetal compromise.

206
00:24:52,323 --> 00:24:59,011
Indeed, some investigators have reported false-positive rates ≥90 percent .

207
00:24:59,011 --> 00:25:09,698
Because healthy fetuses may not move for up to 75 minutes, some have considered that a longer NST duration might increase the positive predictive value of the NST .

208
00:25:09,698 --> 00:25:20,636
In this scheme, either the test became reactive during a period up to 80 minutes or the test remained nonreactive for 120 minutes, which indicated a very ill fetus.

209
00:25:20,636 --> 00:25:28,586
Therefore, prolonged fetal heart rate monitoring is undertaken if an NST remains nonreactive after 40 minutes.

210
00:25:28,586 --> 00:25:32,761
# Nonreactive (Abnormal) Nonstress Tests.

211
00:25:32,761 --> 00:25:39,923
Based on the foregoing, a nonreactive NST is not always ominous and can be seen with a sleeping fetus.

212
00:25:39,923 --> 00:25:45,111
An NST result can also revert to normal as the fetal condition changes.

213
00:25:45,111 --> 00:25:48,711
An example is shown in Figure 20-7.

214
00:25:48,711 --> 00:25:55,136
Instead, a reactive NST result can become abnormal if the fetal condition deteriorates.

215
00:25:55,136 --> 00:25:57,511
Figure 20-7.

216
00:25:57,511 --> 00:26:05,711
Two antepartum fetal heart rate (FHR) tracings in a 28-week pregnant woman with diabetic ketoacidosis.

217
00:26:05,711 --> 00:26:12,011
A. FHR tracing (upper panel) and accompanying contraction tracing (second panel).

218
00:26:12,011 --> 00:26:23,448
Tracing, obtained during maternal and fetal acidemia, shows absence of accelerations, diminished variability, and late decelerations with weak spontaneous contractions.

219
00:26:23,448 --> 00:26:32,661
B. Fetal heart rate tracing shows return of normal accelerations and variability of the fetal heart rate following correction of maternal acidemia.

220
00:26:32,661 --> 00:26:37,536
Some abnormal patterns reliably forecast severe fetal jeopardy .

221
00:26:37,536 --> 00:26:50,848
For example, Devoe and colleagues (1985) concluded that >90 percent of NST results that were nonreactive for 90 minutes or more were associated with significant perinatal pathology.

222
00:26:50,848 --> 00:27:08,123
Specifically, Visser and associates (1980) described a terminal cardiotocogram, which included: (1) baseline variability <5 bpm, (2) absent accelerations, and (3) late decelerations with spontaneous uterine contractions.

223
00:27:08,123 --> 00:27:20,961
These mirrored experiences from Parkland Hospital in which absence of accelerations during an 80-minute recording period in 27 fetuses was associated consistently with evidence of uteroplacental pathology .

224
00:27:20,961 --> 00:27:34,936
The latter included fetal-growth restriction in 75 percent, oligohydramnios in 80 percent, fetal acidemia in 40 percent, meconium in 30 percent, and placental infarction in 93 percent.

225
00:27:34,936 --> 00:27:37,086
Figure 20-8.

226
00:27:37,086 --> 00:27:46,761
Nonreactive nonstress test (left side of tracing) followed by contraction stress test showing mild, late decelerations (right side of tracing).

227
00:27:46,761 --> 00:27:53,011
Cesarean delivery was performed, and the severely acidemic fetus could not be resuscitated.

228
00:27:53,011 --> 00:27:55,836
# Interval Between Testing.

229
00:27:55,836 --> 00:28:03,111
Set originally and arbitrarily at 7 days, the interval between NSTs varies based on indication .

230
00:28:03,111 --> 00:28:18,161
According to the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021a), weekly testing is undertaken in the setting of stable maternal medical conditions such as pregestational diabetes, chronic hypertension, or lupus.

231
00:28:18,161 --> 00:28:24,661
For high-risk conditions such as preeclampsia remote from term, some perform NSTs daily.

232
00:28:24,661 --> 00:28:30,873
Fetal conditions require individualized periodic testing based on the fetal complication.

233
00:28:30,873 --> 00:28:39,686
Fetal-growth restriction is a common indication for which testing intervals vary (Fig. 47-6, p. 829).

234
00:28:39,686 --> 00:28:43,436
# Decelerations During Nonstress Testing.

235
00:28:43,436 --> 00:28:47,586
Fetal movements commonly produce heart rate decelerations.

236
00:28:47,586 --> 00:28:57,323
In one study of 16 near-term fetuses, one half to two thirds of NST tracings had decelerations, depending on the vigor of the fetal motion .

237
00:28:57,323 --> 00:29:03,986
This high incidence of decelerations inevitably makes interpretation of their significance problematic.

238
00:29:03,986 --> 00:29:13,211
Indeed, Meis and coworkers (1986) reported that variable fetal heart rate decelerations during NSTs were not a sign of fetal

239
00:29:13,372 --> 00:29:15,159
compromise.

240
00:29:15,109 --> 00:29:31,434
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021a) has concluded that variable decelerations, if nonrepetitive and brief—less than 30 seconds—do not indicate fetal compromise or the need for obstetrical intervention.

241
00:29:31,434 --> 00:29:41,122
Repetitive variable decelerations—at least three in 20 minutes—have been associated with a greater risk of cesarean delivery for fetal distress.

242
00:29:41,122 --> 00:29:54,022
Decelerations lasting ≥1 minute are reported to have an even worse fetal prognosis (Bourgeois, 1984; Druzin, 1981; Pazos, 1982).

243
00:29:54,022 --> 00:30:06,034
Hoskins and associates (1991) attempted to refine interpretation of tests that showed variable decelerations by adding sonographic estimation of amnionic fluid volume.

244
00:30:06,034 --> 00:30:14,884
The incidence of cesarean delivery for intrapartum fetal distress progressively rose concurrently with the decline of amnionic fluid volume.

245
00:30:14,884 --> 00:30:23,322
Variable decelerations during an NST plus oligohydramnios resulted in a 75-percent cesarean delivery rate.

246
00:30:23,322 --> 00:30:32,897
Fetal distress in labor, however, also frequently developed in those pregnancies with variable decelerations but with normal amounts of amnionic fluid.

247
00:30:32,897 --> 00:30:35,659
Others report similar results .

248
00:30:35,659 --> 00:30:39,097
# False-reactive Nonstress Tests.

249
00:30:39,097 --> 00:30:47,709
In one review of fetal death within 7 days of a reactive NST, the most frequent indication for testing was postterm pregnancy .

250
00:30:47,709 --> 00:30:53,822
The mean interval between testing and death was 4 days, with a range of 1 to 7 days.

251
00:30:53,822 --> 00:31:02,447
The single most common autopsy finding was meconium aspiration, often associated with some type of umbilical cord abnormality.

252
00:31:02,447 --> 00:31:07,297
The authors concluded that acute asphyxia had provoked fetal gasping.

253
00:31:07,297 --> 00:31:15,947
They considered an NST inadequate to preclude an acute asphyxial event, but viewed amnionic fluid volume assessment as valuable.

254
00:31:15,947 --> 00:31:27,834
Other ascribed frequent causes of fetal death despite a false-reactive NST included intrauterine infection, abnormal cord position, fetal malformations, and placental abruption.

255
00:31:27,834 --> 00:31:30,147
Biophysical Profile.

256
00:31:30,147 --> 00:31:36,309
Assessing five specific fetal biophysical variables more accurately predicts fetal health .

257
00:31:36,309 --> 00:31:42,947
These include heart rate acceleration, breathing, movement, tone, and amnionic fluid volume .

258
00:31:42,947 --> 00:31:52,997
In the commonly used biophysical profile (BPP), each normal variable is assigned a score of 2, and abnormal variables are given a score of 0.

259
00:31:52,997 --> 00:31:57,509
Thus, the highest score possible for a normal fetus is 10.

260
00:31:57,509 --> 00:32:06,722
Because fetal breathing and movement are episodic, 30 minutes are allotted to perform a BPP before a score of 0 is assigned to any component.

261
00:32:06,722 --> 00:32:13,934
Figure 20-9 shows color Doppler evidence of amnionic fluid flowing through the nares with fetal breathing.

262
00:32:13,934 --> 00:32:20,572
In one study, BPP scores were higher if a test was performed in late evening compared with the morning .

263
00:32:20,572 --> 00:32:24,684
Narcotics and sedatives can significantly lower the score .

264
00:32:24,684 --> 00:32:26,934
Table 20-2.

265
00:32:26,934 --> 00:32:31,909
Components and Scores for the Biophysical Profile Figure 20-9.

266
00:32:31,909 --> 00:32:38,722
A sagittal color Doppler image displays the movement of amnionic fluid through the nares during fetal breathing.

267
00:32:38,722 --> 00:32:50,397
Using the BPP interpretation and management strategy shown in Table 20-3, Manning and colleagues (1987) evaluated more than 19,000 pregnancies.

268
00:32:50,397 --> 00:32:55,422
Greater than 97 percent of the tested pregnancies had normal results.

269
00:32:55,422 --> 00:33:04,609
They reported a false-normal test rate—defined by an antepartum death of a structurally normal fetus—that approximated 1 per 1000.

270
00:33:04,609 --> 00:33:15,022
The most common identifiable causes of fetal death after a normal BPP score include fetomaternal hemorrhage, umbilical cord accident, and placental abruption .

271
00:33:15,022 --> 00:33:17,322
Table 20-3.

272
00:33:17,322 --> 00:33:32,934
Interpretation of Biophysical Profile Score Subsequently, Manning and associates (1993) published a remarkable description of 493 fetuses in which BPP scores were correlated with umbilical venous blood pH values.

273
00:33:32,934 --> 00:33:39,559
This blood was being obtained via antepartum cordocentesis for other specific fetal indications.

274
00:33:39,559 --> 00:33:50,359
Namely, karyotyping of fetal-growth restricted fetuses constituted 20 percent, and the remainder had alloimmune hemolytic anemia requiring hemoglobin measurement.

275
00:33:50,359 --> 00:34:02,922
As shown in Figure 20-10, a BPP score of 0 was almost invariably associated with significant fetal acidemia, whereas a normal score of 8 or 10 was associated with normal pH.

276
00:34:02,922 --> 00:34:06,209
An equivocal test result—a score of

277
00:34:06,364 --> 00:34:10,376
6—was a poor predictor of an abnormal outcome.

278
00:34:10,326 --> 00:34:17,351
A drop in the BPP score from 2 or 4 down to 0 more accurately predicted an abnormal fetal outcome.

279
00:34:17,351 --> 00:34:23,489
Overall, BPP scores provided poor sensitivity to predict cord blood pH.

280
00:34:23,489 --> 00:34:30,601
Similar studies have concluded the same (Salvesan, 1993; Weiner, 1996).

281
00:34:30,601 --> 00:34:41,939
Kaur and colleagues (2008) performed daily BPPs to ascertain the optimal delivery time in 48 growth-restricted preterm fetuses that weighed <1000 g.

282
00:34:41,939 --> 00:34:49,789
Despite scores of 8 in 27 fetuses and 6 in 13 others, 6 died and 21 were acidemic.

283
00:34:49,789 --> 00:34:52,089
Figure 20-10.

284
00:34:52,089 --> 00:35:01,714
Mean umbilical vein pH (±2 SD) obtained by cordocentesis in relation to fetal biophysical profile score category.

285
00:35:01,714 --> 00:35:04,926
Data from Manning, 1993.

286
00:35:04,926 --> 00:35:08,401
# Modified Biophysical Profile.

287
00:35:08,401 --> 00:35:13,514
BPP performance is labor intensive and requires a trained sonographer.

288
00:35:13,514 --> 00:35:24,201
In response, Clark and coworkers (1989) created an abbreviated BPP, in which an NST was coupled with amnionic fluid volume assessment.

289
00:35:24,201 --> 00:35:30,239
In 2628 singleton pregnancies, the NST was performed twice weekly.

290
00:35:30,239 --> 00:35:42,051
Described in the next section, amnionic fluid volume was measured by the amnionic fluid index (AFI), and values ≤5 cm were considered abnormal.

291
00:35:42,051 --> 00:35:47,226
This abbreviated BPP required approximately 10 minutes to perform.

292
00:35:47,226 --> 00:35:54,039
The authors considered it a superb antepartum surveillance method because no fetuses died unexpectedly.

293
00:35:54,039 --> 00:36:06,339
From another study of 17,429 modified BPPs in 2774 women, investigators concluded that such testing was an excellent fetal surveillance tool .

294
00:36:06,339 --> 00:36:17,526
Miller and associates (1996a) reported results of more than 54,000 modified BPPs performed in 15,400 high-risk pregnancies.

295
00:36:17,526 --> 00:36:25,039
They described a false-negative rate of 0.8 per 1000 and a false-positive rate of 1.5 percent.

296
00:36:25,039 --> 00:36:39,476
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021a) has concluded that the BPP and modified BPP are comparable to other biophysical fetal surveillance approaches in predicting fetal wellbeing.

297
00:36:39,476 --> 00:36:42,026
Amnionic Fluid Volume.

298
00:36:42,026 --> 00:36:52,251
The importance of amnionic fluid volume estimation is indicated by its inclusion into virtually all schemes of fetal health assessment (Frøen, 2008).

299
00:36:52,251 --> 00:37:03,039
Physiologically, diminished uteroplacental perfusion may lead to lower fetal renal blood flow, decreased urine production, and ultimately, oligohydramnios .

300
00:37:03,039 --> 00:37:10,164
Amnionic fluid volume is measured either by the AFI or by the single deepest vertical pocket (DVP).

301
00:37:10,164 --> 00:37:20,514
In one study of more than 1000 women in which both methods were compared, AFI led to a higher rate of oligohydramnios diagnoses and induction of labor.

302
00:37:20,514 --> 00:37:24,489
Despite this, perinatal outcomes were not improved .

303
00:37:24,489 --> 00:37:43,089
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021a) concludes that DVP measurement, as opposed to AFI, is associated with fewer unnecessary interventions but comparable perinatal outcomes (Nabhan, 2008; Reddy, 2014).

304
00:37:43,089 --> 00:37:51,251
An AFI ≤5 cm and a DVP ≤2 cm is considered abnormal.

305
00:37:51,251 --> 00:37:53,439
Doppler Velocimetry.

306
00:37:53,439 --> 00:37:59,001
Blood flow velocity measured by Doppler ultrasound reflects downstream impedance .

307
00:37:59,001 --> 00:38:07,776
This Doppler velocimetry has been used to interrogate the umbilical artery, middle cerebral artery (MCA), and ductus venosus.

308
00:38:07,776 --> 00:38:14,676
With umbilical artery studies, abnormal waveforms correlated with placental villous hypovascularity.

309
00:38:14,676 --> 00:38:25,201
Specifically, of the small placental arterial channels, 60 to 70 percent must be obliterated before the umbilical artery Doppler waveform becomes abnormal.

310
00:38:25,201 --> 00:38:31,351
Such extensive placental vascular pathology has a major effect on fetal circulation.

311
00:38:31,351 --> 00:38:45,964
According to Trudinger (2007), because more than 40 percent of the combined fetal ventricular output is directed to the placenta, obliteration of placental vascular channel increases afterload and leads to fetal hypoxemia.

312
00:38:45,964 --> 00:38:51,826
This in turn leads to ventricular dilation and redistribution of MCA blood flow.

313
00:38:51,826 --> 00:38:58,476
Ultimately, pressure rises in the ductus venosus due to afterload in the right side of the fetal heart .

314
00:38:58,476 --> 00:39:07,826
Clinically, abnormal Doppler waveforms in the ductus venosus are a late finding in the progression of fetal deterioration due to chronic hypoxemia.

315
00:39:07,826 --> 00:39:18,101
To predict placental dysfunction, maternal uterine artery Doppler velocimetry also has been assessed, with the goal to balance stillbirth against the risks of preterm

316
00:39:18,268 --> 00:39:19,855
delivery .

317
00:39:19,805 --> 00:39:22,380
# Umbilical Artery.

318
00:39:22,380 --> 00:39:35,368
The umbilical artery systolic-diastolic (S/D) ratio is considered abnormal if it is above the 95th percentile for gestational age or if diastolic flow is either absent or reversed .

319
00:39:35,368 --> 00:39:45,393
Absent or reversed end-diastolic flow signifies greater impedance to umbilical artery blood flow (Fig. 47-7, p. 830).

320
00:39:45,393 --> 00:39:53,130
It is reported to result from poorly vascularized placental villi and is seen in extreme cases of fetal-growth restriction .

321
00:39:53,130 --> 00:39:59,493
The perinatal mortality rate is increased when absent or reversed end-diastolic flow is present .

322
00:39:59,493 --> 00:40:07,168
Umbilical artery Doppler velocimetry to evaluate fetal health has been extensively assessed in randomized trials.

323
00:40:07,168 --> 00:40:17,530
In one, 1360 women with a high risk for stillbirth underwent either an NST or Doppler velocimetry, and abnormal results prompted labor induction.

324
00:40:17,530 --> 00:40:26,593
More patients in the Doppler group required induction, yet the cesarean delivery rate for intrapartum fetal distress was 4.6 percent.

325
00:40:26,593 --> 00:40:33,405
This rate was significantly lower than the 8.7percent cesarean rate for those receiving an NST .

326
00:40:33,405 --> 00:40:42,655
The authors believed that Doppler assessment, compared with an NST, identified a higher proportion of patients with early placental compromise.

327
00:40:42,655 --> 00:40:49,955
Identified early, these fetuses experienced less distress, which yielded a lower cesarean delivery rate.

328
00:40:49,955 --> 00:41:00,655
Similarly, Yoon and coworkers (1992) found that the BPP and umbilical artery Doppler measurements are comparable indicators of fetal acidosis.

329
00:41:00,655 --> 00:41:09,980
Introduction of Doppler velocimetry as a primary screening test must balance any advantages against its necessary training and associated costs.

330
00:41:09,980 --> 00:41:21,755
In addition, umbilical artery Doppler velocimetry does not effectively predict fetal health in normal pregnancies (Alfirevic, 2015; Page, 2017).

331
00:41:21,755 --> 00:41:33,480
Gonzalez and associates (2007) found that abnormal umbilical artery Doppler findings in a cohort of growth-restricted fetuses were the best predictors of perinatal outcomes.

332
00:41:33,480 --> 00:41:47,780
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021a) has concluded that umbilical artery Doppler velocimetry has not proved valuable as a screening test, except in cases of fetal-growth restriction .

333
00:41:47,780 --> 00:41:50,405
# Ductus Venosus.

334
00:41:50,405 --> 00:42:01,430
Doppler ultrasound can also assess the fetal venous circulation, and as discussed earlier, an abnormal ductus venosus Doppler waveform indicates cardiac dysfunction.

335
00:42:01,430 --> 00:42:07,255
However, its routine use in surveillance of fetal-growth restriction is not recommended .

336
00:42:07,255 --> 00:42:12,968
Ductus venosus Doppler is also used in the staging of twin-twin transfusion syndrome .

337
00:42:12,968 --> 00:42:19,030
Abnormal Doppler indices reflect myocardial dysfunction and predict a poorer outcome .

338
00:42:19,030 --> 00:42:27,805
Additionally, this Doppler method can help monitor fetuses with congenital heart defects and supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) .

339
00:42:27,805 --> 00:42:33,880
Namely, SVT can induce a reversible cardiomyopathy that may lead to hydrops.

340
00:42:33,880 --> 00:42:39,905
Ductus venosus Doppler patterns may aid prediction and monitor improvement following treatment.

341
00:42:39,905 --> 00:42:48,243
Thus, ductus venosus Doppler may have a role in monitoring pregnancies at increased risk for fetal cardiovascular decline .

342
00:42:48,243 --> 00:42:51,068
# Middle Cerebral Artery.

343
00:42:51,068 --> 00:42:56,905
Doppler velocimetry of the MCA is the primary method of detecting fetal anemia .

344
00:42:56,905 --> 00:43:04,780
With fetal anemia, the peak systolic velocity is enhanced due to greater cardiac output and decreased blood viscosity.

345
00:43:04,780 --> 00:43:11,468
However, to detect fetal compromise, Doppler velocimetry of the MCA is not recommended .

346
00:43:11,468 --> 00:43:25,080
In one randomized study of 665 women assigned to modified BPP alone or to modified BPP plus MCA and umbilical artery Doppler velocimetry, pregnancy outcomes did not differ .

347
00:43:25,080 --> 00:43:27,530
# Uterine Artery.

348
00:43:27,530 --> 00:43:33,843
Vascular resistance in the uterine circulation normally declines in the first half of pregnancy.

349
00:43:33,843 --> 00:43:39,218
This stems from trophoblast invasion and remodeling of maternal uterine vessels .

350
00:43:39,218 --> 00:43:47,368
Uterine artery Doppler velocimetry may be most helpful in assessing pregnancies at high risk of uteroplacental insufficiency .

351
00:43:47,368 --> 00:43:57,780
Persistence or development of highresistance patterns has been linked to various pregnancy complications (Sciscione, 2009; Velathur, 2014).

352
00:43:57,780 --> 00:44:07,905
In a study of 30,519 unselected British women, uterine artery velocimetry was assessed at 22 to 24 weeks’ gestation .

353
00:44:07,905 --> 00:44:11,105
The risk of fetal death before 32

354
00:44:11,260 --> 00:44:19,897
weeks, when associated with abruption, preeclampsia, or fetal-growth restriction, was significantly linked to high-resistance flow.

355
00:44:19,847 --> 00:44:25,847
However, technique standards and criteria to define an abnormal test are lacking.

356
00:44:25,847 --> 00:44:33,472
Thus, uterine artery Doppler studies are not considered standard practice in either low- or high-risk populations .

357
00:44:33,472 --> 00:44:35,972
Antenatal Testing Summary.

358
00:44:35,972 --> 00:44:41,897
Despite a continuous evolution of options, the precision of any given method is limited.

359
00:44:41,897 --> 00:44:49,522
Moreover, the wide range of normal biological fetal variation makes interpretation of test results challenging.

360
00:44:49,522 --> 00:44:56,147
This prompts many clinicians to use antenatal testing to forecast fetal wellness rather than illness.

361
00:44:56,147 --> 00:45:03,835
Antenatal testing efficacy was reviewed between 1971 and 1985 at Los Angeles County Hospital .

362
00:45:03,835 --> 00:45:09,385
Nearly 17,000 women underwent antepartum testing of various types.

363
00:45:09,385 --> 00:45:17,697
Fetal surveillance rose from <1 percent of pregnancies in the early 1970s to 15 percent in the mid-1980s.

364
00:45:17,697 --> 00:45:28,635
These authors concluded that such testing was clearly beneficial because the fetal death rate was significantly less in the tested high-risk pregnancies compared with the rate in those not tested.

365
00:45:28,635 --> 00:45:35,397
The study, however, did not consider other innovations incorporated into practice during those years.

366
00:45:35,397 --> 00:45:41,122
Results from Ghana suggest that NSTs may be beneficial in low-resource countries .

367
00:45:41,122 --> 00:45:58,572
Namely, in an observational study of 316 pregnancies complicated by gestational hypertension, women undergoing an NST had a nonsignificant decreased risk for stillbirth compared with those not tested— 3.6 versus 9.2 percent, respectively.

368
00:45:58,572 --> 00:46:06,072
However, the benefits of antenatal fetal testing have not been sufficiently evaluated in randomized trials.

369
00:46:06,072 --> 00:46:15,247
Another important and unanswered question is whether antepartum fetal surveillance identifies fetal asphyxia early enough to prevent brain damage.

370
00:46:15,247 --> 00:46:26,822
Manning and associates (1998) studied the incidence of cerebral palsy in 26,290 high-risk pregnancies managed with serial BPP testing.

371
00:46:26,822 --> 00:46:35,835
These outcomes were compared with those of 58,657 low-risk pregnancies in which antepartum testing was not performed.

372
00:46:35,835 --> 00:46:45,035
The rate of cerebral palsy was 1.3 per 1000 in tested pregnancies compared with 4.7 per 1000 in untested women.

373
00:46:45,035 --> 00:46:57,160
Todd and colleagues (1992) attempted to correlate cognitive development in infants up to age 2 years following abnormal umbilical artery Doppler velocimetry or NST results.

374
00:46:57,160 --> 00:47:03,247
Only abnormal NST results were associated with marginally poorer cognitive outcomes.

375
00:47:03,247 --> 00:47:12,347
These investigators concluded that by the time fetal compromise is diagnosed with antenatal testing, fetal damage has already been sustained.

376
00:47:12,347 --> 00:47:17,210
Low and coworkers (2003) reached a similar conclusion.

377
00:47:17,210 --> 00:47:24,160
Indications for antepartum testing include fetal and maternal conditions that increase the risk for stillbirth .

378
00:47:24,160 --> 00:47:36,622
According to the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021a), a normal antepartum fetal test result is highly reassuring that a stillbirth will not occur within 1 week.

379
00:47:36,622 --> 00:47:44,997
This conclusion was reached after an analysis of reports of stillbirth rates associated with the various antepartum fetal heart rate tests .

